# Target and component map

Define what this run will analyze and leave a coarse map of the code for every later phase to
navigate by. Start from the user's requested component, entry points and focus; do not expand a
narrow request to unrelated services or products.

Establish:

- Included components and languages, the kinds of entry surface in scope (say routes, a public
  API, callbacks or jobs) and who can drive each, and important excluded areas; listing each
  entry point is later work. Record each interface in scope (an API, a service, a protocol)
  under a short name in the `interfaces` of the component exposing it.
- The assumed deployment: process boundaries, exposed interfaces, outbound reach,
  authentication, privileges, and relevant configuration or feature choices.
- The deployment premises later reviews rest on, each `upsert` `{record: {kind: "premise",
  scope: "deployment"}}`, one statement each: every build flag or feature option, actor (each role,
  tenant or other identity: what it may do and reach), platform (OS, architecture, runtime and
  its version), caller behaviour (how an embedding application, client or front proxy calls the
  code, from how many threads at once, and what it limits or checks), concurrency (workers,
  instances) and input bound (sizes, counts, lengths the deployment allows). Set each one the
  user's request settles with `set_status`: `holds` when it admits the condition, `fails` when it
  excludes it. Spell out what a broad request means: "any caller" or "any configuration" admits
  each behaviour the code permits, stated one by one. Leave undecided only what the user has not
  said; ask the parent once here rather than leaving it to each reviewer.
- The revision and dirty-state context, available dependencies, and any inaccessible or
  generated code that limits inspection.

Start from a mechanical inventory of the repository rather than a tour: top-level directories,
file counts by extension, and the build, dependency and deployment manifests, binaries,
interface descriptions, traffic captures and documentation.

```js
{% include "security/scripts/target-inventory.js" %}
```

Every execution environment, deployment or configuration surface and dependency manifest the
inventory shows is in scope by default, including client-side code and background processing.
Excluding one needs the parent's confirmation, recorded as a deployment premise that fails;
only a region with no reachable code is left out silently. An exclusion the request makes is
recorded the same way, stating what it excludes, never that the excluded code's calls into
included code are covered. A documented default is not proof
of the deployed setting, so a non-default option is a deployment premise. Ask once how
target-specific policies affect what counts as a reportable finding (say denial of service, or
which data each actor may see), and record the answer as deployment premises.

Then map the in-scope code into components: a module, a subsystem, a parser or a state machine
each, with code that shares state or a contract kept together. A component's body states its
responsibility, which components it hands work to or takes it from, which ones receive external
input (operator configuration, CI jobs others can trigger and reading state an attacker-driven
flow writes count), its concurrency (what runs its code: threads, tasks, signal handlers, other
processes or instances; the state it shares with other flows or keeps across requests; what
serialises access to it), and what about its boundary is uncertain. Assign files mechanically by
directory or module patterns:

```js
{% include "security/scripts/target-map.js" %}
```

Map what you cannot read (a binary, a deployed service) by what its material shows (route
prefixes, services), one component per binary image, each
body saying what its boundary was inferred from. Code left unassigned is either out of scope with a reason or belongs in a component. The
components mapped before research starts are the run's scope: no later work is scheduled on a
path entering code outside them, so excluded code stays unmapped. The map is
navigation, not coverage: sweeps move files between components as they find them. Delegate
only a scope question, whether a boundary is in or out, to a child launched without a mode,
which records its answer as a deployment premise rather than only replying. Ask the parent when
ambiguity materially changes reachability, authorization or research cost; otherwise state the
narrow working assumption.

When the requested scope includes dependencies, record each locked dependency version a
published advisory affects as a flaw at its manifest entry, naming the advisory and the affected
functions, and state in the manifest's component body that a call into that dependency is a step
into it.

Hand off the component and deployment premise IDs, the interfaces, and exclusions with reasons.
Record each open question as an undecided premise and hand off its ID too. This phase defines
the search boundary; it does not certify that every entry point has been found or authorize live
testing.
